DATA=[{'filename': 'como-ahorar-agua-en-el-jardin.html', 'title_en': 'How to Save Water in the Garden: Practical Ways to Reduce Waste', 'description': 'Learn practical ways to save water in the garden through better watering habits, soil care, plant selection, irrigation maintenance, mulch, and seasonal planning.', 'intro': 'Saving water in the garden does not mean neglecting plants. It means using water more intelligently, reducing avoidable losses, and adapting care to soil, weather, season, and the real needs of each plant.', 'category': 'GARDENING', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>Saving water in the garden does not mean allowing plants to suffer. It means understanding where water is genuinely needed, reducing avoidable losses, and matching irrigation to plants, soil, weather, and season.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Saving water in the garden does not mean allowing plants to suffer. It means understanding where water is genuinely needed, reducing avoidable losses, and matching irrigation to plants, soil, weather, and season. This guide focuses on practical decisions that can be applied in an ordinary home without turning the subject into an expensive or complicated project. The goal is to understand where the biggest opportunities are, make sensible changes, and build habits that remain useful over time.</p>\n<h2>Understand Where Garden Water Goes</h2>\n<p>Saving water begins with understanding how it moves through the garden. Some moisture reaches roots, while some evaporates from exposed soil, runs away from compacted ground, lands on paths, or is lost because irrigation continues longer than necessary. Spend several days observing the garden before changing equipment. Notice which areas dry quickly, which remain damp, where water collects after rain, and which plants show stress first. This simple assessment makes later decisions more precise.</p>\n<p>A garden rarely needs identical watering everywhere. A sunny border exposed to wind can dry much faster than a shaded bed near a wall. Containers usually change moisture more quickly than plants growing in the ground. Dividing the garden into zones according to exposure, soil, and plant needs allows you to direct water where it is useful instead of treating the entire property as one uniform area.</p>\n<h2>Water at a More Effective Time</h2>\n<p>Watering during the cooler part of the day can reduce unnecessary evaporation. Early morning is often practical because plants and soil can receive moisture before the hottest period arrives. Local climate matters, so the best schedule should reflect temperature, humidity, rainfall, restrictions, and the species being grown rather than following one universal rule.</p>\n<p>Timing also means avoiding automatic watering immediately after useful rainfall. If you use an irrigation controller, check whether it can be paused or adjusted according to weather. If you water manually, inspect the soil first. Skipping a session when the root zone is already adequately moist is one of the simplest ways to reduce waste without depriving plants.</p>\n<h2>Check Soil Moisture Before Watering</h2>\n<p>The appearance of the soil surface can be misleading. A dry top layer does not always mean the root zone is dry. Check a little below the surface with a finger or an appropriate moisture-checking method before adding water. Over time, you will learn how quickly different beds and containers dry under different weather conditions.</p>\n<p>This habit is particularly valuable for large pots. Container material, size, drainage, plant density, and sun exposure all affect moisture loss. Instead of watering every pot for the same number of seconds, group containers with similar needs and inspect them regularly. A more informed routine can save water while improving plant care.</p>\n<h2>Improve the Soil's Ability to Use Water</h2>\n<p>Soil structure influences whether water enters gradually, runs across the surface, or remains trapped around roots. Compacted soil may absorb water poorly, while very fast-draining soil may require a different management strategy. Learn the characteristics of your soil before adding amendments, and make improvements gradually according to the plants and local conditions.</p>\n<p>Organic matter can support soil structure when it is appropriate for the site, but more is not automatically better. The objective is a healthy root environment with suitable drainage and moisture retention. Avoid trying to transform the soil overnight. Small, informed improvements combined with good planting choices are usually easier to maintain.</p>\n<h2>Use Mulch Where Appropriate</h2>\n<p>A suitable mulch layer can reduce direct evaporation from bare soil, moderate temperature changes, and help suppress weeds that compete for moisture. Organic or mineral materials may be used depending on the design and plants. Apply mulch appropriately for the site and avoid piling it directly against trunks or plant crowns.</p>\n<p>Mulch does not eliminate the need to monitor moisture. In fact, because the surface may look different after mulching, it becomes even more important to check conditions below it. Treat mulch as one part of a broader water-management plan rather than as a substitute for observation.</p>\n<h2>Choose Plants That Fit the Climate</h2>\n<p>Plant selection has a major influence on long-term water use. Species adapted to local temperatures, rainfall patterns, and seasonal conditions are generally easier to maintain than plants that constantly struggle with the climate. This does not mean a garden must contain only one type of plant; it means choosing varieties whose requirements realistically match the site.</p>\n<p>Before buying, check mature size as well as water needs. A plant that outgrows its position may require frequent pruning and can compete with nearby plants. Good planning reduces both maintenance and resource use. Local horticultural references can help identify species suited to your region.</p>\n<h2>Group Plants With Similar Water Needs</h2>\n<p>Hydrozoning, or simply grouping plants according to similar moisture requirements, makes watering more efficient. Plants that prefer drier conditions should not share the same irrigation routine with plants that require consistently greater moisture. Separating these needs allows each area to receive a more appropriate amount.</p>\n<p>This principle works in containers too. Keeping thirsty plants together and drought-tolerant plants together simplifies inspection and reduces accidental overwatering. It also makes it easier to adjust the garden during seasonal changes because you can modify one zone without affecting every plant.</p>\n<h2>Use Drip Irrigation Carefully</h2>\n<p>Drip systems can deliver water close to the root area and reduce overspray onto paths, walls, and unused ground. Their efficiency still depends on design, maintenance, and scheduling. Emitters can clog, lines can move, and a system can run too long. Inspect it periodically rather than assuming automation guarantees efficiency.</p>\n<p>Position emitters according to the plant and update the layout as plants mature. A young shrub and an established shrub do not occupy the same root area. When a garden changes, irrigation should change with it. Periodic checks can reveal leaks and areas receiving more water than intended.</p>\n<h2>Avoid Watering Paths and Hard Surfaces</h2>\n<p>Watch the garden while irrigation is operating. Sprinklers that repeatedly wet paving, fences, walls, or empty areas are using water without benefiting roots. Adjust heads, repair damaged components, and change the watering pattern where necessary. Small corrections across several zones can produce meaningful savings.</p>\n<p>Runoff is another sign that water is being applied faster than the soil can absorb it. Depending on the site, shorter watering cycles with time for infiltration between them may work better than one long session. The appropriate approach depends on soil, slope, equipment, and local guidance.</p>\n<h2>Collect Rainwater Responsibly</h2>\n<p>Where local rules and conditions allow it, captured rainwater can supplement garden watering. A simple system may collect water from a suitable roof surface into a purpose-designed container. The system should be stable, covered or protected as appropriate, and maintained to avoid contamination, mosquito breeding, overflow problems, and unsafe access.</p>\n<p>Rainwater collection should be planned rather than improvised. Check local regulations, choose equipment intended for the purpose, and direct overflow safely. Even a modest amount of stored rainwater can be useful for selected containers or beds during dry periods, but it should be managed with hygiene and safety in mind.</p>\n<h2>Reduce Competition From Weeds</h2>\n<p>Weeds use water too. Removing unwanted growth before it becomes established reduces competition around garden plants and makes irrigation easier to target. Regular light weeding is generally more manageable than waiting until dense growth covers the soil.</p>\n<p>Do not leave large areas of disturbed bare soil unnecessarily. Depending on the garden, suitable groundcovers or mulch can reduce opportunities for weeds while helping protect the soil surface. Choose solutions that fit the planting design and climate.</p>\n<h2>Pay Extra Attention to Containers</h2>\n<p>Containers can lose moisture quickly because their root zone is limited and exposed to air and heat. Large pots may behave differently from small ones, and dark containers in direct sun can become especially warm. Place containers according to plant requirements and check them individually during hot or windy weather.</p>\n<p>Good drainage remains essential even when conserving water. The objective is not to keep roots permanently saturated but to provide moisture efficiently. Use an appropriate potting medium, make sure drainage holes function, and consider whether the container size is suitable for the mature plant.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Routine to the Seasons</h2>\n<p>Water demand changes throughout the year. A schedule that worked during the hottest month may waste water during cooler or wetter periods. Review irrigation settings and manual habits whenever the season changes significantly. Rainfall, temperature, daylight, wind, and plant growth all influence demand.</p>\n<p>Keep simple notes about major seasonal adjustments. Recording when irrigation was reduced, increased, or paused can help you make better decisions the following year. The goal is not perfect prediction but a routine that responds to real conditions.</p>\n<h2>Create a Weekly Water-Saving Check</h2>\n<p>Once a week, walk through the garden and look for leaking hoses, displaced emitters, wet paving, blocked drainage, stressed plants, and unusually damp soil. This short inspection can prevent small problems from continuing unnoticed for weeks.</p>\n<p>Also review whether new plants or recently moved containers have changed the watering needs of a zone. Gardens are living systems, so an efficient setup is never completely finished. Small adjustments keep the system aligned with the landscape as it develops.</p>\n<h2>Design Future Changes Around Lower Water Demand</h2>\n<p>When adding a new bed or redesigning an old area, consider water use before purchasing plants. Choose suitable species, improve soil where necessary, group similar needs, and decide how watering will reach roots. Planning these details at the beginning is easier than correcting an inefficient layout later.</p>\n<p>Hardscape and shade can also influence water demand. A thoughtful balance of planted areas, permeable surfaces, shade, and circulation can create an attractive garden without requiring every square meter to be heavily irrigated.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should I water my garden?</h3>\n<p>There is no universal schedule. Frequency depends on species, soil, weather, season, root depth, and whether plants are in containers or the ground. Check actual moisture and local guidance rather than watering automatically.</p>\n<h3>Does watering in the morning save water?</h3>\n<p>Cooler conditions can reduce evaporation compared with the hottest part of the day, and morning watering is often practical. Local climate and plant requirements should still guide the final schedule.</p>\n<h3>Is drip irrigation always efficient?</h3>\n<p>It can be efficient when correctly designed and maintained, but leaks, poor emitter placement, and excessive run time can still waste water. Inspect the system regularly.</p>\n<h3>Can I use rainwater in the garden?</h3>\n<p>Rainwater collection can be useful where permitted, but the system should be designed, covered, maintained, and operated safely according to local rules and conditions.</p>\n<h3>What is the easiest first step?</h3>\n<p>Check soil moisture before watering and stop watering hard surfaces. These two habits require little investment and can reveal immediate opportunities to reduce waste.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>An efficient garden uses observation before irrigation. Check soil moisture, group plants with similar needs, improve soil appropriately, maintain irrigation equipment, use mulch where suitable, and adjust routines as seasons change. These habits can reduce unnecessary water use while supporting a healthy, attractive garden. Start with the easiest changes, monitor the results, and improve the system gradually.</p>\n<h2>Create a Simple Garden Water Audit</h2>\n<p>A monthly water audit can be very simple. Walk through each garden zone while the irrigation system or hose is being used and write down anything that looks inefficient. Look for runoff, leaks, overspray, clogged emitters, unusually wet patches, dry areas, and plants whose needs no longer match the zone. Then correct one issue at a time and observe the result.</p>\n<p>If household water bills provide usage information, compare similar periods rather than isolated months because weather and occupancy can change consumption. The purpose of the audit is not to chase a perfect number but to identify avoidable waste and make the garden easier to manage.</p>\n\n<h2>Create a Simple Garden Water Audit</h2>\n<p>A monthly water audit can be very simple. Walk through each garden zone while the irrigation system or hose is being used and write down anything that looks inefficient. Look for runoff, leaks, overspray, clogged emitters, unusually wet patches, dry areas, and plants whose needs no longer match the zone. Then correct one issue at a time and observe the result.</p>\n<p>If household water bills provide usage information, compare similar periods rather than isolated months because weather and occupancy can change consumption. The purpose of the audit is not to chase a perfect number but to identify avoidable waste and make the garden easier to manage.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-ahorrar-energia-en-la-cocina.html', 'title_en': 'How to Save Energy in the Kitchen: Practical Everyday Strategies', 'description': 'Discover practical ways to save energy in the kitchen by improving cooking habits, appliance use, refrigerator care, dishwasher routines, lighting, and maintenance.', 'intro': 'The kitchen is one of the most frequently used areas of a home, and small improvements in cooking and appliance habits can reduce unnecessary energy use without making everyday life more difficult.', 'category': 'KITCHEN & HOME', 'body': "<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<p>Saving energy in the kitchen can reduce unnecessary household consumption without making cooking inconvenient. The most effective approach is to combine sensible appliance use, maintenance, good timing, and simple habits that are repeated every day.</p>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Saving energy in the kitchen can reduce unnecessary household consumption without making cooking inconvenient. The most effective approach is to combine sensible appliance use, maintenance, good timing, and simple habits that are repeated every day. This guide focuses on practical decisions that can be applied in an ordinary home without turning the subject into an expensive or complicated project. The goal is to understand where the biggest opportunities are, make sensible changes, and build habits that remain useful over time.</p>\n<h2>Start With Everyday Cooking Habits</h2>\n<p>Kitchen energy savings often come from repeated small decisions rather than one dramatic change. Think about how often the oven, cooktop, refrigerator, dishwasher, kettle, extractor fan, and small appliances are used. A minor improvement repeated every day can matter more than an occasional large effort. Begin by identifying the appliances you use most frequently.</p>\n<p>Do not try to change every habit at once. Choose a few actions that fit your routine, such as matching pan size to the burner, heating only the water you need, or running the dishwasher with a suitable full load. Once those habits become automatic, add another improvement.</p>\n<h2>Match Pans to the Cooking Zone</h2>\n<p>Using a very small pan on a large cooking zone can waste energy because heat extends beyond the base. Choose cookware that matches the active burner or zone reasonably well. Flat, clean bases also help make good contact where the appliance design depends on it.</p>\n<p>Use lids when the recipe allows. A lid helps retain heat and can bring liquids to temperature more efficiently. Once boiling is reached, many foods do not need the maximum heat setting for the entire cooking time. Reduce the setting when appropriate while maintaining safe cooking temperatures.</p>\n<h2>Heat Only the Water You Need</h2>\n<p>Whether you use a kettle or a saucepan, avoid routinely heating much more water than necessary. Measuring the approximate quantity before heating is a simple habit that can reduce both energy use and waiting time.</p>\n<p>For cooking pasta, vegetables, or other foods, follow appropriate recipe and food-safety guidance rather than assuming that more water is always better. The useful principle is to avoid needless excess while still using enough for proper cooking.</p>\n<h2>Use the Oven Efficiently</h2>\n<p>Ovens use significant energy because they heat a relatively large enclosed space. When practical, cook more than one compatible item during the same session or plan consecutive dishes so that you make useful use of the heat already generated.</p>\n<p>Avoid opening the oven door repeatedly just to check progress. Each opening releases heat and may cause the appliance to work harder to restore the selected temperature. Use the window and light when available, and open the door when the cooking process genuinely requires it.</p>\n<h2>Preheat Only When Necessary</h2>\n<p>Some recipes require a fully preheated oven for texture, rise, or food safety, while others are more flexible. Follow the recipe and appliance guidance rather than automatically running a long preheat for every dish.</p>\n<p>If your oven reaches temperature quickly, learn its normal behavior so you can start preheating at an appropriate time instead of leaving it empty and hot while you continue preparing ingredients. Better timing saves energy without compromising the result.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Appliance for the Portion</h2>\n<p>Heating a large oven for a very small portion may be unnecessary when a suitable smaller appliance can perform the task safely and effectively. Depending on the food, equipment such as a microwave, toaster oven, electric pressure cooker, or other efficient small appliance may be practical.</p>\n<p>Efficiency depends on the appliance, portion, cooking method, and local electricity costs, so there is no single winner for every recipe. The useful habit is choosing equipment in proportion to the task instead of using the largest appliance by default.</p>\n<h2>Keep the Refrigerator Organized</h2>\n<p>A refrigerator works continuously, making good use especially important. Organize food so you can find items quickly instead of leaving the door open while searching. Allow space for the airflow required by the appliance and follow the manufacturer's recommendations for loading.</p>\n<p>Check door seals periodically. A damaged or dirty seal can prevent proper closure. Clean it according to the appliance instructions and replace damaged components when necessary. Also make sure the refrigerator is positioned with the ventilation clearance recommended by the manufacturer.</p>\n<h2>Use Appropriate Refrigerator Temperatures</h2>\n<p>Setting a refrigerator colder than necessary can increase energy use and may not improve food storage. Use temperatures recommended by food-safety authorities and the appliance manufacturer. A simple refrigerator thermometer can help verify conditions if needed.</p>\n<p>Let very hot food cool according to safe food-handling guidance before storage rather than placing large amounts of unnecessary heat into the refrigerator. At the same time, do not leave perishable food at unsafe temperatures. Energy saving should never override food safety.</p>\n<h2>Defrost and Maintain Appliances When Required</h2>\n<p>Some freezers and refrigerators manage frost automatically, while others require periodic defrosting. Excessive frost in equipment that is not designed to operate that way can reduce usable space and affect performance. Follow the manufacturer's maintenance instructions.</p>\n<p>Keep condenser areas, ventilation openings, and accessible filters clean when the manual recommends it. Disconnect power when required for safe maintenance. A clean, properly ventilated appliance is better positioned to operate as designed.</p>\n<h2>Run the Dishwasher Sensibly</h2>\n<p>When you have a dishwasher, use a load size and program appropriate to the dishes and the manufacturer's instructions. Running many nearly empty cycles can use more energy and water than organizing dishes for suitable full loads.</p>\n<p>Eco programs often reduce energy consumption by using lower temperatures and longer cycle times, although exact performance varies by machine. Check the appliance manual and energy information. Avoid unnecessary pre-rinsing when the manufacturer says scraping food residue is sufficient.</p>\n<h2>Use Residual Heat When Appropriate</h2>\n<p>Some electric cooktops and ovens retain useful heat after the active heating element is reduced or switched off. For suitable recipes, this residual heat may help finish cooking. The amount varies greatly by appliance, so learn how your equipment behaves.</p>\n<p>Never use this idea where precise temperature control or continuous heating is required for food safety. It is simply an efficiency technique for dishes that can safely finish with retained heat.</p>\n<h2>Plan Meals Before Turning Appliances On</h2>\n<p>Preparation affects energy use. Chop ingredients, measure liquids, and gather cookware before starting appliances that need to heat. This reduces the chance that a burner or oven sits hot while you search for ingredients.</p>\n<p>Meal planning can also help combine cooking tasks. If two dishes need similar oven temperatures, preparing them in the same session may make better use of the heat. Store leftovers safely and reheat only the portion needed.</p>\n<h2>Keep Cookware in Good Condition</h2>\n<p>Cookware with an appropriate, stable base transfers heat more predictably. Damaged pans may heat unevenly and make cooking harder to control. Use cookware compatible with your cooktop, particularly with induction systems that require suitable magnetic materials.</p>\n<p>Clean pan bases and cooking surfaces according to their care instructions. Good contact and controlled cooking make it easier to use moderate heat rather than compensating for poor performance with unnecessarily high settings.</p>\n<h2>Switch Off What Is Not Being Used</h2>\n<p>Kitchen lights, extractor fans, coffee machines, and other appliances should not remain on without a reason. Some devices use standby power even when they appear inactive. Where practical and consistent with manufacturer guidance, switch equipment off when it is not needed.</p>\n<p>Do not disconnect appliances whose safety, clock, network, or food-storage functions require continuous power. Focus first on devices that are clearly optional and frequently left operating.</p>\n<h2>Improve Kitchen Lighting</h2>\n<p>Replace failed or inefficient lamps with suitable energy-efficient alternatives when appropriate. LED lighting can provide useful task illumination with relatively low electricity consumption and long service life. Choose brightness and color temperature that make food preparation comfortable.</p>\n<p>Task lighting over work surfaces can reduce the need to illuminate the entire kitchen at maximum brightness for a small activity. Natural daylight can also be useful during the day when the room design allows it.</p>\n<h2>Consider Energy Efficiency When Replacing Appliances</h2>\n<p>Do not replace a functioning appliance solely because a newer model exists unless the financial and environmental case makes sense for your situation. When replacement is already necessary, compare energy labels, capacity, expected use, features, warranty, and purchase price.</p>\n<p>An oversized appliance can consume more than you need, while an undersized one may require inefficient repeated use. Choose capacity according to the household's realistic routine. Official energy-label information can make comparisons easier.</p>\n<h2>Build a Simple Weekly Routine</h2>\n<p>Once a week, check the refrigerator door, review food that should be used soon, clean obvious spills, and plan meals around ingredients already available. This reduces food waste as well as unnecessary appliance use.</p>\n<p>Every few months, review filters, seals, ventilation, and maintenance tasks according to appliance manuals. A short checklist prevents easy-to-forget jobs from being postponed indefinitely and keeps the kitchen working efficiently.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What is the easiest way to save energy in the kitchen?</h3>\n<p>Start with repeated habits: use lids, match cookware to burners, heat only the water you need, avoid unnecessary oven-door opening, and switch off equipment that is not being used.</p>\n<h3>Should I replace all my appliances?</h3>\n<p>Usually not simply for the sake of replacement. When an appliance genuinely needs replacing, compare efficiency, capacity, expected use, price, and official energy information.</p>\n<h3>Does an eco dishwasher program really help?</h3>\n<p>Many modern machines design eco cycles to reduce energy and water use, often by operating longer at lower temperatures. Check the manual and energy information for your specific model.</p>\n<h3>Is a microwave always more efficient than an oven?</h3>\n<p>Not for every task. Smaller appliances can be efficient for small portions, but the best choice depends on the food, portion, appliance, and cooking method.</p>\n<h3>Can I save energy without changing how I cook completely?</h3>\n<p>Yes. Most of the techniques in this guide are small adjustments to timing, appliance choice, maintenance, and everyday habits rather than major lifestyle changes.</p>\n<h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>An energy-conscious kitchen does not require complicated rules. Match the appliance to the task, use cookware effectively, organize refrigerator and dishwasher use, maintain equipment according to its manual, and avoid heating or powering things unnecessarily. When an appliance eventually needs replacement, choose an efficient model with a capacity that fits the household. Small improvements repeated consistently are the foundation of meaningful savings.</p>\n<h2>Measure Before Making Expensive Changes</h2>\n<p>If you want to go beyond basic habits, use the energy information available from appliance labels, manuals, utility bills, or suitable household monitoring tools. Measurement helps separate meaningful improvements from changes that sound efficient but have little effect in your particular routine.</p>\n<p>Focus first on appliances used frequently or for long periods. A small saving on something used every day can be more valuable than optimizing a device that operates only occasionally. Keep comfort, food safety, and manufacturer instructions as non-negotiable limits while looking for efficiency.</p>\n\n<h2>Measure Before Making Expensive Changes</h2>\n<p>If you want to go beyond basic habits, use the energy information available from appliance labels, manuals, utility bills, or suitable household monitoring tools. Measurement helps separate meaningful improvements from changes that sound efficient but have little effect in your particular routine.</p>\n<p>Focus first on appliances used frequently or for long periods. A small saving on something used every day can be more valuable than optimizing a device that operates only occasionally. Keep comfort, food safety, and manufacturer instructions as non-negotiable limits while looking for efficiency.</p>\n\n<h2>Measure Before Making Expensive Changes</h2>\n<p>If you want to go beyond basic habits, use the energy information available from appliance labels, manuals, utility bills, or suitable household monitoring tools. Measurement helps separate meaningful improvements from changes that sound efficient but have little effect in your particular routine.</p>\n<p>Focus first on appliances used frequently or for long periods. A small saving on something used every day can be more valuable than optimizing a device that operates only occasionally. Keep comfort, food safety, and manufacturer instructions as non-negotiable limits while looking for efficiency.</p>\n\n<h2>Measure Before Making Expensive Changes</h2>\n<p>If you want to go beyond basic habits, use the energy information available from appliance labels, manuals, utility bills, or suitable household monitoring tools. Measurement helps separate meaningful improvements from changes that sound efficient but have little effect in your particular routine.</p>\n<p>Focus first on appliances used frequently or for long periods. A small saving on something used every day can be more valuable than optimizing a device that operates only occasionally. Keep comfort, food safety, and manufacturer instructions as non-negotiable limits while looking for efficiency.</p>\n"}]

def wc(h): return len(re.findall(r"\b[\w'-]+\b",re.sub(r"<[^>]+>"," ",h)))
print("="*68); print("BLOCK 03 - ARTICLES 03 AND 04"); print("="*68)
for d in DATA:
    n=wc(d["body"])
    if not 2000<=n<=3000:
        print("ERROR -",d["filename"],n,"words"); raise SystemExit(1)
    if not (ART/d["filename"]).exists():
        print("ERROR - file not found:",d["filename"]); raise SystemExit(1)
for d in DATA:
    p=ART/d["filename"]; text=p.read_text(encoding="utf-8",errors="replace")
    b=BACK/d["filename"]
    if not b.exists(): shutil.copy2(p,b)
    text=re.sub(r'<html\s+lang=["\']es["\']','<html lang="en"',text,flags=re.I)
    # Replace first H1 regardless of old Spanish title.
    text=re.sub(r'(<h1[^>]*>).*?(</h1>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title_en"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<title[^>]*>).*?(</title>)',lambda m:m.group(1)+d["title_en"]+" | Home & Garden"+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<meta[^>]+name=["\']description["\'][^>]+content=["\'])[^"\']*(["\'])',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+d["description"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<p[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-intro[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</p>)',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+d["intro"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    text=re.sub(r'(<span[^>]+class=["\'][^"\']*article-category[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</span>)',
                lambda m:m.group(1)+d["category"]+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    pat=r'(<article\b[^>]*class=["\'][^"\']*article-content-full[^"\']*["\'][^>]*>).*?(</article>)'
    text,n=re.subn(pat,lambda m:m.group(1)+"\n"+d["body"]+"\n"+m.group(2),text,count=1,flags=re.I|re.S)
    if n!=1:
        print("ERROR - article-content-full not found:",d["filename"]); raise SystemExit(1)
    common={"Casa & Jardín":"Home & Garden","Publicado:":"Published:","Actualizado:":"Updated:",
            "Inicio":"Home","Categorías":"Categories","Sobre nosotros":"About Us","Contacto":"Contact",
            "Compartir":"Share","Artículos recientes":"Recent Articles","También te puede interesar":"You May Also Like",
            "JARDINERÍA":"GARDENING"}
    for a,b in common.items(): text=text.replace(a,b)
    p.write_text(text,encoding="utf-8")
    print(f'OK - {d["filename"]} -> {wc(d["body"])} words')
print(); print("ARTICLES 03-04: APPROVED"); print("Backup preserved in backup-bloco-03-artigos")
